  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14186732/assets/72c03e75c41470f663cd78ed7ceb81a1c694c36adbb826c4b4d3f52346969be4.png)\n\nFind $m∠D$ in the pentagon $ABCDE$ above.",
  "worked_solution": "A pentagon has $n=5$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon must be \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & ={540}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding up the internal angles and solving for $m∠D,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C+m∠D+m∠E & ={540}^{∘} \\\\ {135}^{∘}+{90}^{∘}+{130}^{∘}+m∠D+{90}^{∘} & ={540}^{∘} \\\\ {445}^{∘}+m∠D & ={540}^{∘} \\\\ m∠D & ={95}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
